Metadata: {"concept":"More distant galaxies correspond to increasingly stretched light waves, visualizing the measured distance-redshift relation behind cosmic expansion.","primary_subject":"hubble_galaxy_distance_redshift","secondary_subjects":["receding_galaxies","stretched_light_waves"],"visual_keywords":[],"composition_hints":["asymmetric_composition","layered_scene"],"scene_type":"diagram","mood":"neutral","palette":"blue_gold"}
This scientifically necessary single diagram shows exactly two simple small galaxy silhouettes: a nearer galaxy with a short tightly spaced light wave and a farther galaxy with a longer more widely spaced light wave, making distance and redshift visually comparable. Broad muted blue and amber forms, no labels, no axes, no arrows, no star field or space backdrop. Use only these essential two paired examples.
